---
import Learn from "../layouts/Learn.astro";
---
<Learn
  title="India Atlas — Learn · Why onions move inflation more than rent"
  description="Learn № 03 — index weights: the CPI basket is not a list but a budget. How the 2011–12 Consumer Expenditure Survey decides what matters, and why your inflation is not the national inflation."
  slug="index-weights"
  kicker="Learn № 03 — Prices · Index weights"
  h1="Why onions move inflation more than <i>rent.</i>"
  lede="The CPI is not a list of prices. It is a budget, in which each item counts in proportion to what households actually spend on it, and the spending shares come from a survey taken in 2011–12."
  meta={[
    ["Concept", "Index weights"],
    ["Illustrated with", "CPI group weights · CES 2011–12"],
    ["Source", "MoSPI, NSO"],
    ["Level", "First principles"],
    ["Reading time", "7 min"],
  ]}
  dateStamp="Evergreen"
  crumbTitle="Index weights"
>

  <!-- § 01 the definition -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The word, defined</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Ask how the national inflation number is built and most people picture a list: three hundred items, each checked monthly, each counting once. The picture is wrong in one decisive way. The basket is not a list but a budget. Rice and rent and bus fares do not get one vote each; they get votes in proportion to the money households actually spend on them.</p>
        <p>Those proportions are the weights. They come from the 2011–12 Consumer Expenditure Survey, a nationwide canvass of what households bought, and they have been frozen into the index ever since. Food and beverages carry 45.86 of every 100 points in the combined index. Housing carries 10.07. Pan, tobacco and intoxicants carry 2.38.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Weight <span class="sub">a share of spending · per cent</span></div>
          <p>An item's share of the average household budget, which is also its share of the index. Price changes are multiplied by weights before they enter the average: a 10 per cent rise in food prices adds about 4.6 points of pressure to the index, while the same rise in pan prices adds 0.2.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the index hears each item in proportion to what you spend on it.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">This is why onions can move the national number and your rent barely registers. Vegetables sit inside the largest group in the budget, so a bad crop can lift the whole index for months. Housing, the expense urban families feel most keenly, carries less than a quarter of food's weight.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · i</span>
          <b>46 paise of every rupee.</b> Food and beverages are 45.86 of the 100 points in the basket. Whatever food does, the index mostly does.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · ii</span>
          <b>The weights are a photograph, not a mirror.</b> They show how India spent in 2011–12, frozen. They describe the average household of that year, which is nobody exactly.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · iii</span>
          <b>One budget, a billion exceptions.</b> Every household runs its own weights. The national number is the average of a spread it never shows.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 the basket -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>What the survey found</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Weights, per cent · CES 2011–12</span></div>
    <div class="break basket">
      <div class="bar" id="basket-bar"></div>
      <div class="labels" id="basket-labels"></div>
      <p class="note">Six groups, frozen from the 2011–12 Consumer Expenditure Survey. Food alone is nearly half the index: the reason a bad onion crop can move the national inflation rate while rents, for most urban households the heaviest bill, move it so little.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 plate : the weights -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>The budget, drawn to scale</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Points of 100</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-wt" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>One hundred points of spending, divided six ways — food speaks loudest</h3>
            <div class="sub">CPI combined group weights · per cent · Consumer Expenditure Survey 2011–12</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-wt">–</div><div class="c">food &amp; beverages · share of the index</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-wt"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source — NSO, Consumer Expenditure Survey 2011–12 · weights frozen into the CPI on base 2012=100 · unchanged since</span>
            <span class="dl">
              <button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Each bar is a group's share of the national budget, out of 100 points. Read them as paise in a rupee: 46 go to food, 28 to the services and odds-and-ends of the miscellaneous group, 10 to housing. The length of a bar is how loudly that group speaks in the national number.</p>
            <p>Two implications follow. A price shock in a long bar moves the index even if everything else is calm. And a price shock in a short bar, however painful for the households it hits, barely reaches the average.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The 2019 case</span>
            <p>In late 2019 onion prices spiked after unseasonal rain ruined the crop, and food inflation climbed to <b id="ex-f19">–</b> by December. Because food carries nearly half the index, the headline followed it to <b id="ex-h19">–</b>, well above the 4 per cent target the RBI is bound to defend.</p>
            <p>A single vegetable, weighted by a billion kitchens, moved the number that Indian monetary policy is legally organised around. That is what a weight of 45.86 means in practice.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 your inflation -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>Why your inflation is not the national inflation</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">A worked example · Dec 2025</span></div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">The national print · Dec 2025</div><div class="v acc" id="n-wx-head">–</div><div class="d">headline CPI, year-on-year</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Food · Dec 2025</div><div class="v" id="n-wx-food">–</div><div class="d">45.86 of every 100 points</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Everything else · Dec 2025</div><div class="v" id="n-wx-nonfood">–</div><div class="d">the other 54.14 points, computed</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">A food-heavy family · Dec 2025</div><div class="v" id="n-wx-fam">–</div><div class="d">60 per cent food, 40 per cent the rest</div></div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:40px">The national number is the average budget made flesh, and no household is average. Take a family that spends 60 per cent of its budget on food, common enough in rural India, and put the remaining 40 per cent into everything else at the national rates. In December 2025 their index rose <b id="wx-fam25">–</b> while the national print read <b id="wx-h25">–</b>: same country, same month, a gap of <b id="wx-gap25">–</b>.</p>
    <p>A year earlier the arithmetic ran the other way. In December 2024 the same family's index rose <b id="wx-fam24">–</b> against a national print of <b id="wx-h24">–</b>, because food, their heaviest bill, was spiking while services were calm. Their inflation ran hotter than the headline in 2024 and cooler in 2025. Both years, it was theirs.</p>
    <p>This is not a flaw in the index. An average must choose a budget, and any budget it chooses will belong to no one. The honest use of the national number is as a common yardstick, not as a description of your kitchen.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- § 05 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">05</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Every item counts equally.”</h3>
          <p>No. Food carries 45.86 of every 100 points; pan, tobacco and intoxicants carry 2.38. A 10 per cent rise in food prices moves the index about nineteen times as much as the same rise in pan prices. An index in which every item counted once would be a different, and stranger, measure.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“My inflation is the national inflation.”</h3>
          <p>Only if your budget is the 2011–12 average, and no one's is. A family spending 60 per cent on food lived at <b id="mr2-fam">–</b> in a month when the national print read <b id="mr2-head">–</b>. The index describes the average household. You live in a specific one.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“The weights are fixed forever.”</h3>
          <p>They are fixed until they are not. The 2011–12 survey still governs every print, and a base revision will eventually replace it with a newer budget, as earlier revisions did. When that happens the weights will shift, and the same prices will print a slightly different index.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. Group weights from the Consumer Expenditure Survey 2011–12, frozen into the CPI on base 2012=100; group indices retrieved via the eSankhyiki API, vintage 2026-08-04.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>The non-food aggregate and the 60 per cent food family are illustrations, computed from the published group indices and the official weights. They are not published series. The national prints shown are the published headline year-on-year rates.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-note">
    <h6>This note</h6>
    <p>Learn № 03 · Prices · version 1.0. Evergreen, written to survive the next print. December 2024 and December 2025 are used as worked history, not news; the chart redraws from the baked data on every build.</p>
    <p>Companion dossier: <a href="/dossiers/cpi/">Dossier 01 · Consumer Price Index</a>.</p>
  </div>

